window.PULSE_CURRENT = {"asOf":"2026-11-28T04:00:00Z","calendar":[{"date":"2026-11-27","event":"Printed: advance October goods deficit $149.5B, up $14.4B, with imports +$21.1B to $365.3B; wholesale inventories +0.9%, retail inventories +0.8%; H.4.1 total assets $6.732T for 25 Nov, down $9.4B; FRED high-yield spread 377 bp for 25 and 26 Nov; Treasury par 10-year 4.94%, 2-year 4.69%; S&P 500 +1.60% to 7,297.49 in the half session; VIX 23.81; Brent $95.44","why":"A broad equity rally against a new high in the credit spread and a 9 bp rise in the 10-year; the net-export drag and inventory build offset in Q4 tracking."},{"date":"2026-11-29","event":"OPEC+ ministerial meeting on 2027 output and capacity estimates","why":"Three sources told CNBC that Q1 2027 targets stay unchanged with Iraq negotiating a higher ceiling; Brent at $95.44 after a 3% weekly gain on the Jazan strike, with the IEA projecting a Q4 surplus"},{"date":"2026-11-29","event":"Spanish snap election","why":"The week's European political event; a weak result adds to euro-area risk ahead of the 17 Dec ECB"},{"date":"2026-11-30","event":"Final November session; FRED postings for the 27 Nov VIX after 23.81 and high-yield spread after 377 bp; FRED broad dollar index update after 123.60 for 20 Nov; Chicago PMI; $95B 3-month and $82B 6-month bill auctions","why":"Sets the November month-end closes on the trend tabs; the 27 Nov spread print says whether credit followed the equity rally"},{"date":"2026-12-01","event":"ISM Manufacturing PMI, November, consensus 55; JOLTS, consensus 7.5M; construction spending; GDPNow update after 2.9%; Huang closed-door interview with three House committees; Marvell earnings after the close","why":"After October's 55.5 with new orders 55.5, production 59.0 and prices paid 75.2; the flash PMI was 54.5 and the Philadelphia Fed 6.3, so a print under 52 would say the regional weakness has spread; new orders under 50 moves the regime to Slowdown"},{"date":"2026-12-02","event":"ADP private payrolls, consensus 80K","why":"First read on November hiring before Friday's report; October ADP and the 165K payroll print diverged"},{"date":"2026-12-03","event":"ISM Services PMI, November; initial claims for the week to 28 Nov after 189K; H.4.1 for 2 Dec; Huang Senate Banking testimony","why":"After October's 54.5 with new orders down 3.9 points to 55.9 and prices at 73.0; the flash services PMI was 54.7; claims under 200K keep the labour market in the healthy band"},{"date":"2026-12-04","event":"November jobs report, LSEG consensus 90K and 4.1% unemployment; preliminary December UMich sentiment after 51.5","why":"After October's 165K with 84K of upward revisions; claims fell to 189K and continuing claims to 1.697M in the survey week, so a print above 100K is the base case; under 50K moves the regime to Slowdown, and the print decides January pricing from 60.1%: a strong report pushes it toward 70-75%, a weak one makes it a coin flip; last payroll print before the FOMC and the Fed is in blackout"},{"date":"2026-12-04","event":"Section 232 solar tariffs take effect","why":"Adds to the tariff pass-through the Fed hawks cite"},{"date":"2026-12-09","event":"FOMC decision, with the summary of economic projections","why":"A 25 bp hike to 4.00-4.25% priced at 99.2% on FedWatch; the projections show the terminal rate and whether January is in the dots"},{"date":"2026-12-10","event":"November CPI","why":"The day after the FOMC decision, so the committee hikes on the October print; first month with Brent in the $90s; the October release had no 12-month changes"},{"date":"2026-12-14","event":"White House committee hearing on Fed Governor Cook","why":"Moved from 5 Nov at Cook's request, so it now falls after the 8-9 Dec FOMC; the panel is Hassett, Lucas and Sonderling, and the Supreme Court ruled on 29 June that removal needs cause and a chance to respond"},{"date":"2026-12-16","event":"November retail sales","why":"Second consumer check after the 0.6% October control group and flat real October PCE"},{"date":"2026-12-17","event":"ECB decision, a 25 bp hike priced at 92% per Barchart; November housing starts and permits after 1.297M","why":"Eurozone inflation rose to 4.0% in October, the highest since September 2023; permits are the one leading indicator outside sentiment that has turned down"},{"date":"2026-12-18","event":"Bank of Japan decision, a hike priced at 79% per Barchart; Japan services PPI printed 4.2% y/y, the highest since 1990","why":"A BoJ hike with the US 2-year at 4.69% narrows the carry that holds the yen near 160 and is the rates channel back into Treasuries."},{"date":"2026-12-29","event":"Conference Board consumer confidence, December, after 103.3","why":"The survey that just jumped 11.4 points; a reversal would say the post-midterm lift did not survive the equity fall, since the November cut-off was 17 Nov"},{"date":"2027-01-05","event":"Written comments due on the USTR Section 301 excess-capacity tariffs; US-China truce expires 10 Jan; hearings from 25 Jan","why":"Final action on up to 20% for China and 15% to 35% for 13 others is unlikely before late January 2027"}],"conflicts":[{"a":"Q3 GDP revised to 4.3% with real final sales to private domestic purchasers at 5.8%, claims at 189K tying the year's low, continuing claims down 95K to 1.697M, core capital goods orders up 1.2% and durables ex-transportation up 11.4% on a year ago on FRED's October level, new home sales up 14.2% to 701K, Conference Board confidence at 103.3, eight of twelve Beige Book districts growing, wholesale and retail inventories up 0.9% and 0.8%","b":"Real October spending flat with real disposable income up 0.5%, GDPNow at 2.9%, the advance goods deficit widening $14.4B to $149.5B on an import surge, UMich at 51.5, 18.5 points under our 70 line, permits at a five-year low of 1.297M, the flash composite PMI at a four-month low of 54.6, the Philadelphia Fed at 6.3, the LEI down 0.3%, LSEG's payroll consensus at 90K","read":"The hard data and the labour market say the economy entered Q4 at or above trend; the consumer surveys, housing, the nowcast and the import surge say the hand-off is slower and more of it is being met from abroad. The framework weights the leading set, which is softer but still above 54 on the national ISMs, so Expansion holds with the slowdown logged.","trigger":"ISM new orders under 50 on 1 Dec, services under 50 on 3 Dec or payrolls under 50K on 4 Dec would move the regime to Slowdown; ISM manufacturing at or above the 55 consensus and payrolls above 100K would close the case for Q4 near trend. GDPNow under 2.5% after 1 Dec would say Q4 is losing the Q3 momentum."},{"a":"The S&P 500 up 1.60% to 7,297.49 with all 11 sectors up, more than four in five stocks higher, the equal-weight index up about 1.3%, the best week since April 2025 at +5.6%, the semiconductor index up 4.69% and 9.25% on the week, the VIX down to 23.81, Anthropic back above its IPO price","b":"FRED's high-yield spread at 377 bp for 25 and 26 Nov, a new high for this leg above the 376 bp of 20 Nov, CoreWeave's 2030 notes near an 18.6% yield and Nvidia and Oracle CDS at records, Nvidia up only 0.80% and 8.8% under Monday on an S&P CreditWatch negative, the index still 6.7% under the record and 26 points under the 7,323.63 line","read":"Equities say the AI drawdown is over and credit says the financing of the build is still being repriced; the two usually resolve with credit leading. Under the framework a broad equity rally with widening spreads is a markets event that stays Risk-off until the spread turns, and the 27 Nov spread print is the first piece of evidence either way.","trigger":"A 27 Nov spread print under 370 bp with the VIX under 22 would say credit is following equities; a print above 380 bp with the index rising would be the divergence that preceded the 19-20 Nov falls. An S&P 500 close above 7,323.63 with the VIX under 20 and the spread under 340 bp moves the posture to Neutral; a close under 7,000 or a Nasdaq-100 close 20% under its peak reopens the leg. Huang's 1 and 3 Dec testimony and the 90-day CreditWatch are the Nvidia triggers."},{"a":"Core PCE at 3.3%, the highest since November 2023, headline 3.8%, GDP 4.3%, claims 189K, the Beige Book's input costs outpacing selling prices, PPI 6.5%, the par 2-year back up 8 bp to 4.69%, FedWatch at 99.2% for December, Waller's call for 50 bp, Brent up 3% on the week","b":"January pricing down to 60.1% from 67.4% on a hot PCE day and three hikes by March down to 35.2% from 51.7%, 2s10s at 25 bp near the June low, real spending flat, GDPNow 2.9%, the goods deficit widening on imports, UMich year-ahead inflation expectations at 3.7%, Bessent warning the Fed risks over-tightening, gold futures at $3,668.50","read":"The committee is responding to the inflation it has and the market agrees on December, but the futures have trimmed the path beyond it on the same days the data ran hot, which says the market reads the equity correction and the flat consumer as the tightening the Fed would otherwise have to deliver. The 10-year at 4.94% is back up but still under 5.00%, so the long end is pricing a slower economy, not a higher terminal rate.","trigger":"A 2-year above 4.80% says the inflation arithmetic is unchanged and January is back above 70%; under 4.50% says the market expects the correction to do the Fed's work. The 9 Dec projections show the committee's terminal rate; a 10-year over 5.10% afterward restores the Risk-off line on rates, under 4.75% with equities falling would be a growth scare."},{"a":"A projectile killing a worker and halting units at Aramco's 400,000 b/d Jazan refinery, the Houthi claim on Ras Tanura with four drones intercepted, ballistic missiles at Riyadh and a cruise missile near Jeddah intercepted overnight into 26 Nov, a drone attack in Kuwait killing two Italian technicians, UKMTO's tanker hit east of Khasab, the IRGC's 26 Nov claim that two tankers hit mines near Khasab, Israel saying it is ready to strike Iran at any moment, Kushner saying all options stay open","b":"Brent settling down 1.2% at $95.44 on the day the Jazan damage was confirmed, up only about 3% on the week from a three-month low, oil down nearly 20% for November, the IEA's Q4 surplus, Gulf shipments recovering, US crude stocks up a third week, three sources saying OPEC+ holds Q1 2027 targets on 29 Nov with Iraq pushing for a higher ceiling, ANZ saying $100 needs proof that deliverable supply is constrained","read":"The war is hitting Saudi, Kuwaiti and Emirati infrastructure and the strait is being mined by Iran's own account, and the oil market still prices a surplus because flows have recovered and the US strike decision is parked past January. At $95.44 the energy channel is neither helping US inflation nor forcing the Fed's hand; it is a GCC fiscal and risk-premium story first and a US inflation story only above $100.","trigger":"Brent above $100 is the relief line lost and $115 the trigger; Aramco confirming a lasting outage at Jazan or Ras Tanura, a sunk tanker at the blockade line or a resumption of US mass strikes would reprice both within a session. OPEC+ on 29 Nov sets the 2027 supply response; a surprise cut would be the bullish signal the market is not pricing."},{"a":"Micron up 7.60%, Sandisk 15.96% and the semiconductor index up 9.25% on the week, China's CSI semiconductor index up a record 17% on 27 Nov, Dell's $54.9B quarter, core capital goods orders up 1.2% and 11.2% on the year, durables ex-transportation up 11.4% on a year ago, the Beige Book's data-centre demand, HP up 11.49% on guidance","b":"Nvidia up only 0.80% to $150.49 and 8.8% under Monday on S&P's CreditWatch negative citing key-person risk, CoreWeave CDS at a record 15.34% with its 2030 notes near an 18.6% yield, 20 of 24 high-yield data-centre bonds wider since 28 Sep per the 27 Nov digests, Section 301 tariffs of up to 15% to 25% proposed on Taiwan, Korea and Japan with semiconductors excluded only under Section 232, Huang's congressional interviews from 1 Dec","read":"Demand for AI hardware is intact in every hard-data series and the memory names are being bought on it, while the market prices the financing of the build and the anchor company's governance as the risk. The framework reads this as a markets event until capex plans or orders change; the growth score moves on orders, not on CDS.","trigger":"A hyperscaler capex cut, a Nvidia guidance reduction, an S&P downgrade within the 90-day CreditWatch or an SEC enforcement action would move the growth score; a semiconductor index close above its 18 Nov level would say the governance shock is priced; a CoreWeave default event would move credit, not growth."}],"drivers":[{"date":"2026-11-27","src":"AP via myMotherLode; Milton Berg Edge; Stock Titan; The Company Chronicle; Trading Economics","tag":"Markets","title":"S&P 500 rises 1.60% to 7,297.49 in the 1 pm half session, up 5.6% on the week, the best week since April 2025, and 6.6% for the year; Nasdaq +2.31% to 24,129.50, up 6.7% on the week; Dow +1.18% to 50,774.94; Russell 2000 +1.71% to 2,750.30; all 11 sector funds up, more than four in five S&P 500 stocks higher, median stock +0.86%, equal-weight about +1.3%; semiconductor index +4.69% to 9,186.71, up 9.25% on the week; Sandisk +15.96%, Coherent +11.92%, Micron +7.60%, Marvell +7.34%, Western Digital +7.22%; Nvidia +0.80% to $150.49, 8.8% under Monday's $165.05; Anthropic +10.98% to $91.88, above its $83 IPO price; Tesla +5.22%, AMD +4.56%, Amazon +3.96%, Broadcom +3.82%, Meta +3.13%, SpaceX +4.83%; Vertex −5.24% on an FDA review extension; Brazil ETF +9.81% after Lula names Bradesco chairman Trabuco finance minister, real to 5.53 from 5.88; VIX 23.81, down 33.7% on the week; DXY 104.04, +0.36%; gold futures $3,668.50, +1.96%; bitcoin just under $60,000; China's CSI semiconductor index +17%, a record one-day gain, Kospi +4.34%, Nikkei +2.11%; the index is 6.7% under the 6 Oct record and 3.7% down for November.","url":"https://mymotherlode.com/news/national/11344241/how-major-us-stock-indexes-fared-friday-11-27-2026.html"},{"date":"2026-11-27","src":"US Treasury; Investing.com; Federal Reserve H.4.1; Census Bureau; FRED; The Glass Desk","tag":"Policy","title":"Treasury par curve posts 27 Nov at 4.69%, 4.74%, 4.82%, 4.94%, 5.36% and 5.33% for the 2-, 5-, 7-, 10-, 20- and 30-year, up 8 to 9 bp across the curve as the haven bid fades, 2s10s 25 bp; CME FedWatch per Investing.com prices 99.2% for a 25 bp hike on 9 Dec with 50 bp at zero, 60.1% for two hikes by 27 Jan, down from 67.4% on 24 Nov, and 35.2% for three by 17 Mar, down from 51.7% on 16 Nov; Robinhood prediction markets price 92% for December; the Fed blackout begins 28 Nov; the H.4.1 shows total assets down $9,408M to $6,732,044M on 25 Nov; the advance October goods deficit widens $14.4B to $149.5B with exports +$6.7B to $215.8B and imports +$21.1B to $365.3B, wholesale inventories +0.9% and retail inventories +0.8%; FRED posts the high-yield spread at 377 bp for both 25 and 26 Nov, above the 376 bp peak of 20 Nov, and VIXCLS at 26.80 for 26 Nov; GDPNow stays at 2.9% until 1 Dec; LSEG's payroll consensus for 4 Dec is 90K with 4.1% unemployment.","url":"https://www.investing.com/analysis/feds-december-rate-hike-is-priced-in-but-january-remains-uncertain-200692473"},{"date":"2026-11-27","src":"CNBC via The Circuitry and Political.org; Bloomberg via Rigzone; Gulf News","tag":"Geopolitics","title":"Brent settles down $1.13 or 1.2% at $95.44 after touching its highest since 9 Nov, up about 3% on the week, its first weekly gain in November; WTI settles down $0.60 at $83.08 with no Thursday settlement; a projectile hit Aramco's 400,000 b/d Jazan refinery on 26 Nov, killing a Nepali worker, injuring others and temporarily halting some operations per the Saudi Energy Ministry, with the coalition blaming a Houthi launch platform in Hodeidah that it says it destroyed along with 50 targets in Sanaa, Hodeidah and Saada; Saudi forces intercepted ballistic missiles aimed at Riyadh and a cruise missile and drone near Jeddah overnight into 26 Nov; a drone attack on a facility in Kuwait killed two Italian Rheinmetall technicians servicing an air-defence system and injured a Kuwaiti and a Pakistani, Iran's armed forces deny involvement and von der Leyen calls for more pressure on Iran; UKMTO reports a tanker struck 18 nautical miles east of Khasab on 25 Nov and ADNOC confirms a crew member from the 17 Nov attack died; Kushner says Trump keeps all options open and has met Iranian representatives several times; Israel's Defrin says the IDF is ready to strike Iran at any moment; three sources tell CNBC that OPEC+ will keep Q1 2027 targets unchanged on 29 Nov while Iraq's Khudair says Iraq is negotiating a higher ceiling; ANZ says Brent can clear $100 without another escalation only if deliverable supply proves constrained; the IEA projects a Q4 surplus and Gulf shipments are recovering.","url":"https://www.rigzone.com/news/wire/deadly_houthi_strike_at_jazan_refinery_halts_some_operations-27-nov-2026-185180-article/"},{"date":"2026-11-26","impact":"The Hormuz premium is being rebuilt from a low base while the US is closed; Brent is $3.43 under the $100 relief line.","src":"Reuters via The Nation and WKZO","tag":"Geopolitics","title":"Brent settles up $1.96, or 2.07%, at $96.57 with no official WTI settlement on Thanksgiving after Iran's Revolutionary Guards say two tankers, one a supertanker carrying more than 2 million barrels, exploded after hitting sea mines north of Oman's Khasab on an unauthorised route and that the force is using heavy firepower to keep the strait closed to pressure the US economy; UKMTO reports an explosion from an unknown projectile near a tanker 50 nautical miles west of Dubai; the Saudi-led coalition says it intercepted four drones aimed at Ras Tanura and satellite images show smoke; EIA crude stocks rise 1.1M barrels to 418.9M against 1.5M; two OPEC+ sources expect targets unchanged through Q1 2027 at the 29 Nov meeting.","url":"https://www.nationthailand.com/news/world/40073981"},{"date":"2026-11-26","impact":"The tariff shock is sized at the deal caps rather than 2025 levels; Europe recovers part of the two-week fall without a US session.","src":"Korea Times; Reuters via Longbridge","tag":"Trade","title":"European stocks rally as USTR says the proposed Section 301 tariffs are capped at the levels agreed in the past year's trade deals, 15% including MFN for Korea, Japan, Taiwan, the EU and Switzerland, 20% for China rising to 40% if the truce breaks down, 20% or 35% for Vietnam and 17% or 25% for India depending on a deal, 18% for Cambodia, Indonesia, Malaysia, Thailand and Bangladesh and 10% for Singapore, with no stacking on forced-labor or Section 232 duties and a public hearing on 25 January 2027; Greer says the caps are no surprise to partners, China's embassy rejects the excess-capacity finding without threatening retaliation and Wyden vows to use every tool to end the tariffs; the Stoxx 600 closes up 1.8% at 636.98 with Germany, Spain and Italy up more than 2%, miners, autos and defence up more than 3%, and Hernandez de Cos's nomination to head the ECB read as continuity.","url":"https://www.koreatimes.co.kr/world/20261126/us-proposes-tariffs-over-excess-production-retains-15-cap-for-korea-japan-eu"},{"date":"2026-11-26","impact":"Back-to-back hikes are now the futures' base case into a market that has already tightened; the Treasury is publicly leaning the other way.","src":"CME FedWatch via Longbridge; TheStreet via Yahoo Finance; Saxo; Trading Economics","tag":"Policy","title":"CME FedWatch prices 97.4% for a 25 bp hike on 9 Dec and 0.6% for a hold, with the January cumulative distribution at 62.7% for 50 bp and 37% for 25 bp, per Longbridge; TheStreet recaps Bessent's 22 Nov CBS warning that the Fed risks over-tightening after very hawkish remarks and a substantial tightening in financial conditions, his 20 Nov call not to embrace a big hike, Waller's view that 50 bp should be considered, Bank of America's 25 bp base case with 50 bp more likely than before and JPMorgan's December plus two more in early 2027; Saxo puts the Treasury $511.1B from the $41.1T debt limit as of 24 Nov; the Census final October permits figure is 1.297M, up from 1.295M preliminary and down 7.2% on the month.","url":"https://longbridge.com/en/news/306945956"},{"date":"2026-11-25","impact":"Growth is stronger and inflation higher than the advance data showed; the December hike is settled and the regime holds at Expansion.","src":"BEA; Department of Labor; Census Bureau; Federal Reserve Beige Book; Atlanta Fed; Stock Titan; Reuters via WTAQ and Investing.com","tag":"Data","title":"October core PCE rises 0.3% m/m and 3.3% y/y against 3.2%, the highest since November 2023, from 3.1%; headline 0.3% and 3.8% from 3.6%, in line; personal income +0.7% on one-time Medicare rebates, real disposable income +0.5%, nominal spending +0.3%, real spending flat, saving rate 4.1% from 3.8%; Q3 GDP revised up to 4.3% from 3.4% against 3.5%, real final sales to private domestic purchasers 5.8%, real GDI 4.4%, corporate profits +$206.0B and +21.3% y/y, Q3 PCE prices 2.1% and core 2.5% unrevised; initial claims −13K to 189K against 200K, tying the year's low, prior revised to 202K, four-week average 196K, continuing claims −95K to 1.697M from a revised 1.792M; durable goods +0.2% to $346.7B against about 0.6% after +2.0% revised, ex-transportation +0.5%, core capital goods orders +1.2% against 0.7% with September revised to 0.8%, core shipments +0.6%; new home sales +14.2% to 701K against 650K with September cut to 614K, median price $391,300, −3.2% y/y, supply 8.0 months; GDPNow cut to 2.9% from 3.2%; Beige Book: eight of twelve districts growing slight to moderate, four flat, employment up slightly, wages up moderately, prices up moderately with input costs outpacing selling prices, consumers trading down, investment strong on data centres.","url":"https://www.bea.gov/sites/default/files/2026-11/pi1026.pdf"},{"date":"2026-11-25","impact":"A near bear-market open in the Nasdaq-100 was bought, but the AI complex kept falling and its credit is now the stress point; posture stays Risk-off.","src":"AP via myMotherLode; CappNotes; KuCoin; Yahoo Finance; Stock Analysis","tag":"Markets","title":"S&P 500 falls 0.25% to 7,182.78 after being down 3.2% intraday, a ninth loss in ten; Nasdaq −0.62% to 23,585.80 after a 4.6% intraday fall, Nasdaq-100 −0.64% to 26,046.90 after nearing a 20% drawdown; Dow +0.33% to 50,182.95 after a fall of more than 1,100 points; Russell 2000 +0.71% to 2,704.06; breadth 370 up against 128 down, seven of eleven sectors up, materials +2.07%, technology −0.83%, equal-weight ETF +0.83%, semiconductor index −0.65%; Nvidia −3.91% to $149.30 after a near-9% intraday fall as S&P Global puts its AA rating on CreditWatch negative citing board independence, key-person risk and China; Oracle −5.13%, Micron −4.53%, Amazon −3.14%, Ambarella −19.77%, Nutanix −15.19%; HP +11.49% on guidance, Analog Devices +6.44%, Texas Instruments +4.55%, Meta +3.4%, IBM +3.34%; CoreWeave five-year CDS +209 bp to a record 15.34%, Nvidia, Google, Oracle and SpaceX CDS at records; Anthropic falls to $74 early and closes up about 1% near $83; VIX 28.29, +7.32%; dollar index 103.71, −0.10%; gold futures −0.71% to $3,534.70 after an intraday break below $3,500; bitcoin about $60,100 from $58,000; Dell reports $54.9B revenue after the close; AP puts the S&P 500 up 3.9% on the week and 4.9% for the year; Burry says his book is almost entirely long.","url":"https://mymotherlode.com/news/national/11340405/how-major-us-stock-indexes-fared-wednesday-11-25-2026.html"},{"date":"2026-11-25","impact":"The long end rallied through a hot core PCE print, which reads as the bond market pricing the correction as a growth drag; the front end is anchored on December.","src":"US Treasury; investinglive; CappNotes; financialmarkets.com; FRED","tag":"Policy","title":"Treasury par curve posts 25 Nov at 4.61%, 4.65%, 4.74%, 4.85% and 5.24% for the 2-, 5-, 7-, 10- and 30-year, down 2 to 6 bp, 2s10s 24 bp; the market 10-year closes at 4.847%, down 5.7 bp, an eighth straight fall that began before the data; the $44B 7-year auction stops at 4.708%, a 1.0 bp tail against the 4.698% when-issued and a 0.1 bp average, bid-to-cover 2.43 against 2.48, directs 33.19% against 24.64%, indirects 58.20% against 63.64%, dealers 8.61%; CME FedWatch per CappNotes 97.8% for a 25 bp December hike, Kalshi 93%, Polymarket 94%, 50 bp near 1.5% to 2.5%, Polymarket's January contract 53.5 cents from 50.5; FRED posts the 24 Nov high-yield spread at 372 bp, +9 bp, and the 24 Nov VIX at 26.36; Freddie Mac 30-year mortgage 7.21% from 7.28%; no Fed speakers before 8-9 Dec.","url":"https://investinglive.com/news/us-treasury-sells-44-billion-of-70-or-notes-at-a-high-yield-of-4-708/"},{"date":"2026-11-25","impact":"The 2025 tariff wall the Supreme Court struck down is being rebuilt under Section 301 on a 2027 timetable; the pass-through channel the Fed hawks cite is now written down.","src":"USTR via Baker McKenzie Global Import Blog; CNBC; Yahoo Finance","tag":"Trade","title":"USTR publishes Section 301 determinations against 14 economies in the structural excess capacity investigation and proposes tariffs: up to 20% on China net of the July forced-labor tariff, 10% on Annex A goods and Annex B exempt, with up to 40% if engagement is inadequate; 15% on the EU and up to 15% on Japan, Korea, Switzerland and Taiwan net of MFN and existing 301 duties, with 20% to 25% alternatives; up to 20% on Singapore, 25% on India with 17% under a deal, 35% on Vietnam with 20% under a deal, 18% on Indonesia, Malaysia, Thailand, Cambodia and Bangladesh; Section 232 goods, including steel, aluminum, copper, autos, wood, patented pharmaceuticals and semiconductors, excluded for all but China; no determinations yet for Mexico and Norway; written comments due 5 Jan 2027, hearings from 25 Jan at the ITC, final action unlikely before late January; the US-China truce expires 10 Jan.","url":"https://globalimportblog.bakermckenzie.com/2026/11/25/us-ustr-proposes-section-301-tariffs-on-14-economies-over-structural-excess-capacity/"},{"date":"2026-11-25","impact":"A claimed hit on the largest Saudi refinery lifted oil 5.9% in two days from a three-month low; the GCC risk premium is rising faster than the oil price and OPEC+ sets 2027 supply on 29 Nov.","src":"Reuters via The Nation; CappNotes","tag":"Geopolitics","title":"Brent settles up $3.21, or 3.5%, at $94.61 and WTI up $1.63, or 2%, at $83.68 after the Houthis claim attacks on Saudi Arabia's Ras Tanura refinery, the kingdom's largest and part of a major crude export terminal, and on Riyadh's financial district; a source tells Reuters the refinery came under attack and Aramco does not comment, with the effect on output unclear; the IRGC says it stopped a container ship on an unauthorised route near Hormuz on 24 Nov; EIA crude stocks +1.1M barrels to 418.9M against 1.5M expected, exports −1.2M b/d to 3.3M; Libya's Sharara pipeline reopens after a brief valve closure; Iraq and Kazakhstan press for higher capacity estimates before the 29 Nov OPEC+ meeting on 2027 quotas, the UAE no longer bound by limits per LSEG; short covering before Thanksgiving per PVM.","url":"https://www.nationthailand.com/news/world/40073934"},{"date":"2026-11-24","impact":"The AI leg of the rally reversed while the rest of the market rose; posture stays Risk-off on the VIX and the spread.","src":"AP via myMotherLode; KuCoin; Hammerstone via Investrade; Reuters via GV Wire","tag":"Markets","title":"S&P 500 falls 0.50% to 7,201.09, an eighth loss in nine sessions, Nasdaq −1.48% to 23,733.54, Dow +0.85% to 50,018.70, Russell 2000 +0.57% to 2,684.94; Philadelphia semiconductor index −7.88% with all 30 components down, Nvidia −5.86% to $155.38, Analog Devices −17.37%, Texas Instruments −11.25%, Micron −10.97%, HPE −16.31% on margin warnings; health care +2.20%, staples +1.80%, technology −3.70%; Anthropic closes at $81.91, −6.54%, under its $83 IPO price; VIX 26.36; gold futures −2.45% to $3,561.90, silver −5.54%; dollar −0.44% from a 20-month high; Fear & Greed 18; AP puts the S&P 500 up 4.2% on the week and 5.2% for the year.","url":"https://mymotherlode.com/news/national/11337878/how-major-us-stock-indexes-fared-tuesday-11-24-2026.html"},{"date":"2026-11-24","impact":"The second-tier surveys turned up while UMich stays 18.5 points under the 70 line; credit retraced a quarter of its widening.","src":"Conference Board via PR Newswire; Trading Economics; TheStreet Pro; FRED","tag":"Data","title":"Conference Board consumer confidence jumps 11.4 points to 103.3 in November against 92.2 expected and a revised 91.9, the highest since January 2025; present situation 132.8, +13.7, expectations 83.6, +9.9, jobs plentiful 30.8% from 26.2%, jobs hard to get 18.4% from 21.4%, 12-month inflation expectations 5.7%, down a second month; McCormick cites lower uncertainty after the midterms and continued frustration with high prices, survey cut-off 17 Nov; Richmond Fed index rises to 3 from −2 against 2, new orders 2 from −8, shipments 2, employment 6, prices paid 7.59% from 7.12%; Redbook sales +11.5% y/y; FRED posts the 23 Nov high-yield spread at 363 bp, −13 bp, and the 23 Nov VIX at 26.62.","url":"https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/us-consumer-confidence-surged-in-november-302949447.html"},{"date":"2026-11-24","impact":"Reopens the tariff pass-through channel the Fed hawks cite and a trade risk to the EU, Japan, Korea and China spillovers.","src":"WSJ via Investing.com and Yahoo Finance","tag":"Trade","title":"WSJ reports the administration is preparing new tariffs on China, Japan, South Korea, the 27 EU members and others, with USTR Greer able to unveil findings of the industrial-subsidy probe covering China, the EU and 14 other economies as soon as this week; officials aim to replicate the broad 2025 levies the Supreme Court struck down in February, with rates capped at 15% or lower for partners with deals, the steepest on China, escalation triggers if overcapacity is not curbed, Vietnam and India possibly facing higher duties under existing agreements, steel, aluminum and autos excluded, and the new duties stacking on the July 10% to 12.5% forced-labor tariffs on 60 economies.","url":"https://finance.yahoo.com/economy/policy/articles/white-house-prepares-sweeping-tariffs-135524943.html"},{"date":"2026-11-24","impact":"Attacks reached Riyadh's financial centre a second time while oil bounced from a three-month low; the GCC risk premium is rising faster than the oil price.","src":"Arab News; Al Jazeera; Reuters via The Nation","tag":"Geopolitics","title":"A Houthi drone strikes a tower in Riyadh's King Abdullah Financial District, injuring 11 people, two Saudis, two Indians, three Nepalis and four Egyptians, the second attack on the district in under a week after 19 Nov damage to the PIF tower; coalition spokesman Al-Malki promises a firm response; UKMTO reports three ships hit by unidentified projectiles transiting Hormuz eastbound on 23 Nov, all continuing with crew accounted for, and Iranian state media say a container ship struck a mine on 24 Nov; Ghalibaf says Iran will not hold talks until Washington meets its seven conditions and warns of a wider battlefield; the WSJ reports a US probe of Iranian cyberattacks on up to 50 tankers; January Brent settles up 2.27% at $91.40 after an $88.10 low and WTI up 2.35% at $82.05; Deutsche Bank puts October supply 1.7M b/d above demand.","url":"https://www.arabnews.com/saudi-arabia/11-injured-in-houthi-attack-on-tower-in-riyadhs-financial-district-3013857"},{"date":"2026-11-24","impact":"Domestic demand replaced foreign demand at the belly; the market prices a steady 25 bp path rather than a 50 bp jump.","src":"investinglive; TheStreet Pro; US Treasury; Reuters via GV Wire","tag":"Policy","title":"The $70B 5-year auction stops at 4.719%, a 0.3 bp tail against the 4.716% when-issued and a 0.9 bp average, a 13th straight tail per TheStreet, with bid-to-cover 2.37 against 2.31, directs 36.7%, the highest since records began in 2008, indirects 54.3%, the lowest since March 2020, dealers 9.01%; the Treasury par curve posts 24 Nov at 4.63%, 4.69%, 4.91% and 5.30% for the 2-, 5-, 10- and 30-year; CME FedWatch per Reuters prices 98% for a December hike and 66% for a further hike in January with the 50 bp scenario wiped out; no Fed speakers until the 8-9 Dec meeting; the $44B 7-year auction is 25 Nov.","url":"https://investinglive.com/news/the-us-treasury-sells-70-billion-of-5-year-notes-at-a-high-yield-of-4-719/"}],"fed":{"label":"Tightening","note":"October core PCE at 3.3% on the year against 3.2%, the highest since November 2023, Q3 GDP at 4.3% and claims at 189K leave the committee hiking: CME FedWatch per Investing.com on 27 Nov priced 99.2% for a 25 bp hike on 9 Dec to 4.00-4.25% with the 50 bp option at zero, 60.1% for two hikes by the 27 Jan meeting, down from 67.4% on 24 Nov, and 35.2% for three by 17 Mar, down from 51.7% on 16 Nov. The blackout began on 28 Nov, so the 4 Dec jobs report, with a 90K consensus, is the last input before the meeting. The par 2-year rose 8 bp to 4.69% on 27 Nov. Bessent said on 22 Nov that the Fed risks over-tightening; Waller has called for 50 bp in December or a faster 2027 pace per Reuters. The Fed's balance sheet fell $9.4B to $6.732T in the week to 25 Nov."},"headline":"The S&P 500 rose 1.60% to 7,297.49 in the 27 Nov half session, its best week since April 2025 at +5.6%, with the Nasdaq up 2.31% to 24,129.50, the Dow up 1.18% to 50,774.94 and the Russell 2000 up 1.71% to 2,750.30, as memory and AI chip stocks rebounded with Sandisk up 15.96% and Micron 7.60%, Nvidia only 0.80% to $150.49, and Anthropic up 10.98% to $91.88 above its $83 IPO price; the VIX fell to 23.81; the Treasury par curve posted the 2-year at 4.69%, the 10-year at 4.94% and the 30-year at 5.33%, up 8 to 9 bp, with FedWatch at 99.2% for a December hike and 60.1% for two by January; FRED's high-yield spread printed 377 bp for 25 and 26 Nov, a new high for this leg; Brent settled down 1.2% at $95.44, up about 3% on the week, after a Houthi projectile killed a worker and halted some units at Aramco's 400,000 b/d Jazan refinery and a drone attack in Kuwait killed two Italian technicians, with OPEC+ expected to hold Q1 2027 targets on 29 Nov; the advance October goods deficit widened to $149.5B; the Fed's balance sheet fell to $6.732T; regime Expansion, posture Risk-off, the Fed Tightening.","marketClose":"Fri 27 Nov 2026","notes":["Index closes for 27 Nov are the AP's half-session figures and match Milton Berg Edge and STL News; The Glass Desk quotes 7,298.34 for the S&P 500 and 50,780.64 for the Dow from Yahoo, which are not used. The VIX close of 23.81 is Milton Berg Edge's and The Glass Desk's; The Company Chronicle quoted 24.01 at about 1:30 pm Eastern before the final print, and FRED's VIXCLS was posted to 26 Nov at 26.80 when opened on 28 Nov, a Thanksgiving value that matches Saxo's 05:00 CET quote from the global trading hours session.","Yields are the Treasury par curve posted for 27 Nov: 2-year 4.69%, 5-year 4.74%, 7-year 4.82%, 10-year 4.94%, 20-year 5.36%, 30-year 5.33%, confirmed on the Treasury's 2026 table; FRED's DGS10 is posted to 25 Nov. The 2s10s of 25 bp is derived from the par curve.","FRED's ICE BofA high-yield spread prints 3.77% for both 25 and 26 Nov, so the November point on both tabs moves to 377 bp, a new high for this leg; the 27 Nov value is expected on 30 Nov. The Fed's H.4.1 of 27 Nov gives total assets of $6,732,044M for 25 Nov, which both tabs carry; FRED's WALCL was still posted to 18 Nov when opened.","Brent on the Latest tab is the 27 Nov ICE settlement of $95.44 per CNBC as carried by The Circuitry and Political.org, which agree on the figure; the Reuters wire pages were not reachable. FRED's Brent spot series is posted to 24 Nov at $91.80, which the trend tabs carry. The DXY close of 104.04 and +0.36% is Trading Economics' for 27 Nov against CappNotes' 103.71 for 25 Nov; The Glass Desk quotes 104.02. FRED's broad dollar index is posted to 20 Nov at 123.60.","FedWatch odds of 99.2% for December, 60.1% for two hikes by 27 Jan and 35.2% for three by 17 Mar are CME FedWatch as cited by Investing.com for early 27 Nov; The Glass Desk cites 97% and 98% for December and 66% for January from Reuters and Yahoo earlier in the week, and Robinhood prediction markets priced 92%.","Durables ex-transportation on the trend tabs is now computed from FRED's ADXTNO levels: October 226,726 against 203,472 a year earlier gives 11.43%; September is corrected to 11.10% on the revised 225,631 level and August to 11.28% on 224,569. Core PCE is computed from FRED PCEPILFE at 3.26% for October against the BEA's published 3.3%.","The advance goods trade and inventory figures are from the Census advance economic indicators report of 27 Nov; no consensus was confirmed for them. GDPNow was not updated on 27 Nov."],"posture":{"label":"Risk-off","note":"Two of the three conditions still fail after the 27 Nov rally. The VIX closed at 23.81, down 4.48 from 28.29 on 25 Nov and down 33.7% on the week, but above the under-20 condition; FRED's high-yield spread is 377 bp for both 25 and 26 Nov, 5 bp above the 24 Nov print and a new high for this leg, 37 bp above the 340 bp condition; the Treasury par 10-year rose 9 bp to 4.94% and stays under 5.00% for a fifth session, the one condition met. The S&P 500 rose 1.60% to 7,297.49, its best week since April 2025 at +5.6%, with all 11 sectors up and more than four in five stocks higher, but the close is still 6.7% under the 6 Oct record and 26 bp under the 7,323.63 line that would say 20 Nov was the low. Neutral needs a VIX close under 20, a 10-year par close under 5.00% and a spread under 340 bp together."},"regime":{"label":"Expansion","note":"The 27 Nov half session added only the advance October goods deficit, which widened $14.4B to $149.5B as imports rose $21.1B to $365.3B against a $6.7B rise in exports, with wholesale inventories up 0.9% and retail inventories up 0.8%, so the coincident case from 25 Nov stands: Q3 GDP revised to 4.3%, claims at 189K tying the year's low, core capital goods orders up 1.2% and FRED's durables ex-transportation level now 11.4% above a year ago. The leading set is still above 54 on ISM manufacturing 55.5, new orders 55.5 and services 54.5, against permits at a five-year low of 1.297M and UMich at 51.5, 18.5 points under our 70 line, with GDPNow at 2.9% until 1 Dec. The regime holds at Expansion; ISM on 1 and 3 Dec and payrolls on 4 Dec, with a 90K LSEG consensus, are the next tests."},"schema":1,"sections":[{"id":"leading","rows":[{"change":"+1.0","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":48},{"l":"Dec 25","v":47.9},{"l":"Jan 26","v":52.6},{"l":"Feb 26","v":52.4},{"l":"Mar 26","v":52.7},{"l":"Apr 26","v":52.7},{"l":"May 26","v":54},{"l":"Jun 26","v":53.3},{"l":"Jul 26","v":55.6},{"l":"Aug 26","v":54.6},{"l":"Sep 26","v":54.5},{"l":"Oct 26","v":55.5}],"latest":"55.5","name":"ISM Manufacturing PMI","next":"1 Dec","period":"Oct 2026","prior":"54.5","read":"Rose to 55.5 in October from 54.5 against a 54.5 to 54.7 consensus, a tenth month above 50 and the first rise since July’s 55.6. Production rose to 59.0 from 56.7, the highest since November 2021, inventories to 51.4 from 48.6 and imports to 52.9, while employment slipped to 52.4, backlogs to 54.6 and prices paid to 75.2 from 77.9, still high. ISM counted 12 industries growing and 3 contracting; the S&P Global final PMI was 54.9, revised down from the 56.0 flash. The S&P Global flash manufacturing PMI for November was 54.5 on 23 Nov against 54.9 expected and 54.9 in October, with new orders growing at the weakest pace since July and employment up, a softer but still expansionary cross-check ahead of the 1 Dec ISM.","ref":50,"refLabel":"50 = expansion line","signal":"good","signalWord":"Expansion, beat","src":"ISM via PR Newswire","trend":"up","trendWord":"Accelerating","url":"https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/manufacturing-pmi-at-55-5-october-2026-ism-manufacturing-pmi-report-302925617.html"},{"change":"+0.2","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":47.4},{"l":"Dec 25","v":47.4},{"l":"Jan 26","v":57.1},{"l":"Feb 26","v":55.8},{"l":"Mar 26","v":53.5},{"l":"Apr 26","v":54.1},{"l":"May 26","v":56.8},{"l":"Jun 26","v":56},{"l":"Jul 26","v":56.7},{"l":"Aug 26","v":53.7},{"l":"Sep 26","v":55.3},{"l":"Oct 26","v":55.5}],"latest":"55.5","name":"ISM Manufacturing New Orders","next":"1 Dec","period":"Oct 2026","prior":"55.3","read":"Our critical leading signal edged up to 55.5 from 55.3, a second monthly gain after August’s 53.7, and export orders rose to 51.2 from 50.9. Production at 59.0 is now running ahead of orders and backlogs fell to 54.6 from 56.4, so factories are working down order books rather than adding to them. Demand is holding in the mid-50s, not accelerating.","ref":50,"refLabel":"50 = expansion line","signal":"good","signalWord":"Expansion","src":"ISM via PR Newswire","trend":"up","trendWord":"Firm","url":"https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/manufacturing-pmi-at-55-5-october-2026-ism-manufacturing-pmi-report-302925617.html"},{"change":"−0.4","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":52.4},{"l":"Dec 25","v":53.8},{"l":"Jan 26","v":53.8},{"l":"Feb 26","v":56.1},{"l":"Mar 26","v":54},{"l":"Apr 26","v":53.6},{"l":"May 26","v":54.5},{"l":"Jun 26","v":54},{"l":"Jul 26","v":54.1},{"l":"Aug 26","v":55.4},{"l":"Sep 26","v":54.9},{"l":"Oct 26","v":54.5}],"latest":"54.5","name":"ISM Services PMI","next":"3 Dec","period":"Oct 2026","prior":"54.9","read":"Eased to 54.5 in October from 54.9 against a 54.8 consensus, a 28th month of expansion and a second monthly decline from August’s 55.4. Business activity fell 2.7 points to 53.8 and new orders 3.9 points to 55.9, while employment rose to 51.4 from 50.1, export orders to 55.2 from 46.9 and backlogs to 58.0; prices paid slipped to 73.0 from 74.0, and ISM’s chair said more pricing pressure is on the way if oil and tariff costs are not mitigated. All ten sub-indexes were above 50 for the first time since February and 15 industries grew against 2. No divergence from manufacturing at 55.5, but services demand is cooling from a high level while factory output accelerates. The S&P Global flash services PMI for November was 54.7 on 23 Nov against 55.7 expected and 57.9 in October, pulling the composite to 54.6, a four-month low, while Williamson still reads the surveys as about 3% annualised Q4 growth; the 3 Dec ISM services print tests whether the October slide to 54.5 extends.","ref":50,"refLabel":"50 = expansion line","signal":"good","signalWord":"Expansion, orders slower","src":"ISM via PR Newswire","trend":"down","trendWord":"Easing","url":"https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/services-pmi-at-54-5-october-2026-ism-services-pmi-report-302928112.html"},{"change":"+3.5","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":52.9},{"l":"Jan 26","v":56.4},{"l":"Feb 26","v":56.6},{"l":"Mar 26","v":53.3},{"l":"Apr 26","v":49.8},{"l":"May 26","v":44.8},{"l":"Jun 26","v":49.5},{"l":"Jul 26","v":55.2},{"l":"Aug 26","v":51.7},{"l":"Sep 26","v":48.1},{"l":"Oct 26","v":48},{"l":"Nov 26","v":51.5}],"latest":"51.5","name":"UMich Consumer Sentiment","next":"4 Dec (Dec prelim)","period":"Nov 2026 final","prior":"48.0","read":"Final November reading of 51.5, revised up from the 51.0 preliminary, against 48.0 in October and consensus of 51.5, up 7.3% on the month and the highest in three months. Current conditions were 49.4 from 46.7 and expectations 52.9 from 48.8. Year-ahead inflation expectations fell to 3.7% from 4.7% in October and 4.3% at mid-month, the lowest since March, and long-run expectations to 3.3% from 3.5%. The AP cites easing gasoline prices and post-midterm business optimism; the survey says the index is still 9% below February, before the Iran war. It is 18.5 points under our 70 threshold and 6.7 above the May record low of 44.8, so the read stays weak. The December preliminary prints 4 Dec.","ref":70,"refLabel":"70 = our weak/moderate line","signal":"bad","signalWord":"Weak, below 70","src":"University of Michigan via Trading Economics","trend":"up","trendWord":"Three-month high","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/united-states/consumer-confidence"},{"change":"−7.2%","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":1.414},{"l":"Dec 25","v":1.482},{"l":"Jan 26","v":1.393},{"l":"Feb 26","v":1.54},{"l":"Mar 26","v":1.363},{"l":"Apr 26","v":1.423},{"l":"May 26","v":1.41},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1.374},{"l":"Jul 26","v":1.433},{"l":"Aug 26","v":1.403},{"l":"Sep 26","v":1.398},{"l":"Oct 26","v":1.297}],"latest":"1.297M","name":"Building Permits","next":"17 Dec (Nov data)","period":"Oct 2026 final","prior":"1.398M","read":"The final October figure is 1.297M, revised up from the 1.295M preliminary of 18 Nov, a 7.2% fall from September's 1.398M per the Census revision of 25 Nov via Trading Economics, the largest drop in seven months and a third straight monthly decline; the preliminary print came against a 1.38M consensus. Single-family permits fell 8.2% to 777K, the lowest since January 2023, and five-plus-unit permits fell 5.8% to 520K, a four-month low. This is the lowest reading in the five-year history on the trend tab, below the 1.347M of August 2025. Starts rose 9.9% to 1.309M on a 25.5% jump in five-plus-unit buildings, so the pipeline is multifamily while the single-family side shrinks; pending home sales fell 4.7% to a record-low index of 63 and the NAHB index is at 25. On our roughly 10-month lead this points to weaker construction jobs and tighter credit by mid-2027, and it is the first leading indicator outside sentiment to turn down. New home sales rose 14.2% to 701K in October against 650K, a ten-month high, with the median price down 3.2% on the year to $391,300 and supply at 8.0 months, so builders are clearing inventory with price cuts rather than pulling permits.","signal":"bad","signalWord":"Lowest in five years; third monthly fall","src":"Census Bureau via Trading Economics","trend":"down","trendWord":"Largest drop in seven months","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/united-states/building-permits"}],"sub":"Forward-looking. Read these first.","title":"Leading indicators"},{"id":"coincident","rows":[{"change":"−13K","hist":[{"l":"5 Sep","v":207},{"l":"12 Sep","v":198},{"l":"19 Sep","v":198},{"l":"26 Sep","v":199},{"l":"3 Oct","v":198},{"l":"10 Oct","v":200},{"l":"17 Oct","v":195},{"l":"24 Oct","v":195},{"l":"31 Oct","v":191},{"l":"7 Nov","v":202},{"l":"14 Nov","v":202},{"l":"21 Nov","v":189}],"latest":"189K","name":"Initial Jobless Claims","next":"3 Dec","period":"Week to 21 Nov","prior":"202K, revised from 203K","read":"Initial claims fell 13,000 to 189,000 in the week to 21 November against 200,000 expected, tying the lowest reading of the year and a level last seen in the late 1960s, and the prior week was revised down 1,000 to 202,000; the four-week average fell to 196,000. Continuing claims fell 95,000 to 1.697 million in the week to 14 November from a revised 1.792 million, reversing the previous week's jump, with the insured unemployment rate back at 1.1%. The two weeks above 200,000 now read as noise rather than a turn, and the print argues for a payroll number above the 65K October consensus-style estimates on 4 December. The November average on the trend tab is 197,700 from the three weeks so far. The release was moved up a day for Thanksgiving; the next is 3 December.","signal":"good","signalWord":"Low; 4-week average 196K","src":"Department of Labor","trend":"down","trendWord":"Ties the year's low","url":"https://www.dol.gov/ui/data.pdf"},{"change":"+105K","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":41},{"l":"Dec 25","v":-17},{"l":"Jan 26","v":160},{"l":"Feb 26","v":-156},{"l":"Mar 26","v":214},{"l":"Apr 26","v":148},{"l":"May 26","v":63},{"l":"Jun 26","v":31},{"l":"Jul 26","v":-10},{"l":"Aug 26","v":186},{"l":"Sep 26","v":60},{"l":"Oct 26","v":165}],"latest":"+165K","name":"Nonfarm Payrolls","next":"4 Dec","period":"Oct 2026","prior":"+60K","read":"October payrolls rose 165K against a 65K consensus and a prior 12-month average of 48K, and August was revised up 53K to 186K and September up 31K to 60K, 84K higher combined. Professional and business services added 70K, health care 42K, transportation and warehousing 25K and manufacturing 19K; government was little changed. Average hourly earnings rose 0.4% on the month to $38.00 and 3.1% on the year, and the workweek held at 34.4 hours. The three-month average is 137K, still under our 200K to 300K healthy range but no longer the weak reading in the set: it now agrees with claims at 189K, ADP at 86K and job openings at 7.523M. Part-time for economic reasons rose 339K to 4.8M, the one soft line in the report.","ref":200,"refLabel":"200K = our healthy floor","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Beat; below 200K benchmark","src":"Bureau of Labor Statistics","trend":"up","trendWord":"Rebound, revised up","url":"https://www.bls.gov/news.release/empsit.nr0.htm"},{"change":"−0.1 pt","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":4.5},{"l":"Dec 25","v":4.4},{"l":"Jan 26","v":4.3},{"l":"Feb 26","v":4.4},{"l":"Mar 26","v":4.3},{"l":"Apr 26","v":4.3},{"l":"May 26","v":4.3},{"l":"Jun 26","v":4.2},{"l":"Jul 26","v":4.1},{"l":"Aug 26","v":4.1},{"l":"Sep 26","v":4.2},{"l":"Oct 26","v":4.1}],"latest":"4.1%","name":"Unemployment Rate","next":"4 Dec","period":"Oct 2026","prior":"4.2%","read":"Fell to 4.1% in October from 4.2%, with 7.1M unemployed, and has stayed between 4.1% and 4.3% since March. Participation was 61.9% and the employment-population ratio 59.3%, both little changed since January. Long-term unemployed were 2.0M, or 27.6% of the total, and part-time for economic reasons rose 339K to 4.8M. The Fed’s September projection has the rate averaging 4.1% in Q4, which this print matches, so the labor side gives the FOMC no reason to pause the hike path.","signal":"good","signalWord":"Low, stable range","src":"Bureau of Labor Statistics","trend":"down","trendWord":"Back to 4.1%","url":"https://www.bls.gov/news.release/empsit.nr0.htm"},{"change":"0.0 pt","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":0.47},{"l":"Dec 25","v":1.24},{"l":"Jan 26","v":0.37},{"l":"Feb 26","v":1.25},{"l":"Mar 26","v":1.13},{"l":"Apr 26","v":1.47},{"l":"May 26","v":1.8},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1.14},{"l":"Jul 26","v":0.71},{"l":"Aug 26","v":0.32},{"l":"Sep 26","v":0.46},{"l":"Oct 26","v":0.5}],"latest":"+0.5%","name":"Durable Goods ex-Transportation","next":"Dec (Nov data)","period":"Oct 2026, m/m","prior":"+0.5%","read":"New orders excluding transportation rose 0.5% in October, matching September, per the Census advance report of 25 Nov, while headline orders rose 0.2% to $346.7B against about 0.6% expected after a 2.0% September, revised from 1.8%; transportation fell 0.3% as civilian aircraft fell with Boeing at 165 orders. Core capital goods orders rose 1.2% against 0.7% expected and 11.2% on the year, with September revised up to 0.8% from 0.6%, and core shipments rose 0.6% after 1.3%; machinery rose 1.8%. FRED's ADXTNO level for October is now posted at $226,726M against $203,472M in October 2025, up 11.4% on the year, with September revised to $225,631M, so the year-on-year series on the trend tabs is filled through October. The capex cycle is still running on AI: business equipment spending has grown at double-digit rates for three quarters and the Beige Book's Cleveland district said demand is concentrated in data-centre buildouts, with the Q3 GDP revision adding 0.44 points from investment.","ref":0,"refLabel":"0 = no change","signal":"good","signalWord":"Capex cycle intact","src":"Census Bureau advance report; FRED ADXTNO","trend":"flat","trendWord":"Steady; core capex +1.2%","url":"https://www.census.gov/manufacturing/m3/adv/current/index.html"},{"change":"−1.1 pt","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":-0.18},{"l":"Dec 25","v":0.45},{"l":"Jan 26","v":-0.45},{"l":"Feb 26","v":0.86},{"l":"Mar 26","v":-0.17},{"l":"Apr 26","v":0.83},{"l":"May 26","v":0.06},{"l":"Jun 26","v":0.2},{"l":"Jul 26","v":0.23},{"l":"Aug 26","v":-0.1},{"l":"Sep 26","v":0.66},{"l":"Oct 26","v":-0.4}],"latest":"−0.4%","name":"Industrial Production","next":"Dec (Nov data)","period":"Oct 2026, m/m","prior":"+0.7%","read":"Fell 0.4% in October against +0.1% expected, the first decline since August, per the Fed’s G.17 on 17 Nov. The miss is weather and energy, not factories: utilities fell 4.3% after a 4.4% rise in September and mining fell 0.8%, while manufacturing rose 0.3% against 0.2% expected, though manufacturing ex-motor vehicles edged down 0.1%. August was revised to −0.1% from +0.1% and September held at +0.7%. Output is 2.1% above a year ago and capacity utilization fell to 76.4%, 3.0 points below its long-run average. The hard data still match the ISM at 55.5 on the manufacturing side; the headline weakness is in the volatile components. Q3 GDP was revised up to 4.3% on 25 Nov with investment contributing 2.10 points and real GDI at 4.4%, so the production side of the economy entered Q4 stronger than the advance estimate showed.","ref":0,"refLabel":"0 = no change","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Miss; manufacturing +0.3%","src":"Federal Reserve G.17","trend":"down","trendWord":"Utilities-led drop","url":"https://www.federalreserve.gov/releases/g17/current/default.htm"}],"sub":"Confirmation of what the leading set signalled.","title":"Coincident indicators"},{"id":"policy","rows":[{"change":"+0.2 pt","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":2.72},{"l":"Dec 25","v":2.87},{"l":"Jan 26","v":2.94},{"l":"Feb 26","v":2.96},{"l":"Mar 26","v":3.11},{"l":"Apr 26","v":3.08},{"l":"May 26","v":3.16},{"l":"Jun 26","v":3.04},{"l":"Jul 26","v":3},{"l":"Aug 26","v":3.05},{"l":"Sep 26","v":3.14},{"l":"Oct 26","v":3.26}],"latest":"3.3%","name":"Core PCE, y/y","next":"Dec (Nov data)","period":"Oct 2026","prior":"3.1%","read":"Core PCE rose 0.3% in October and 3.3% on the year from 3.1% against 3.2% expected, the highest since November 2023; the FRED index gives 3.26% from 131.312 against 127.165 a year earlier, with September revised to 3.14% and August to 3.05%, so core has been at or above 3.0% for eight months. Headline PCE rose 0.3% on the month and 3.8% on the year from 3.6%, in line. Personal income rose 0.7%, lifted by one-time Medicare rebate payments, real disposable income 0.5% and the saving rate rose to 4.1% from 3.8%, while real spending was flat, so households banked the income rather than spending it. The Q3 PCE price index was 2.1% annualised and core 2.5%, unrevised, which shows the re-acceleration is a second-half story. The print is the last PCE before the 8-9 Dec FOMC; November CPI is 10 Dec.","ref":2,"refLabel":"2% = Fed target","signal":"bad","signalWord":"Above 2% target, rising; beat","src":"Bureau of Economic Analysis","trend":"up","trendWord":"Highest since Nov 2023","url":"https://www.bea.gov/sites/default/files/2026-11/pi1026.pdf"},{"change":"−0.2 pt","hist":[{"l":"Sep 25","v":3.02},{"l":"Nov 25","v":2.7},{"l":"Dec 25","v":2.65},{"l":"Jan 26","v":2.39},{"l":"Feb 26","v":2.43},{"l":"Mar 26","v":3.29},{"l":"Apr 26","v":3.78},{"l":"May 26","v":4.17},{"l":"Jun 26","v":3.46},{"l":"Jul 26","v":3.3},{"l":"Aug 26","v":3.35},{"l":"Sep 26","v":3.74}],"latest":"+0.4%","name":"CPI, y/y","next":"10 Dec","period":"Oct 2026, m/m","prior":"+0.6%","read":"October CPI rose 0.4% on the month against 0.3% expected after 0.6% in September; core rose 0.3% against 0.2%, matching September. Food rose 0.5%, energy 0.7% with gasoline up 1.0% seasonally adjusted, shelter 0.3% with owners’ equivalent rent 0.3%. The BLS published no 12-month changes because October 2025 prices were not collected during the shutdown, so the chart stops at September’s 3.7% headline and 2.6% core. October PPI, released 13 Nov, rose 0.3% on the month and 6.5% on the year, the most since November 2022, with core PPI at 5.0%, so the pipeline is still pushing into consumer prices. November CPI is 10 Dec, the day after the FOMC.","ref":2,"refLabel":"2% = Fed target","signal":"bad","signalWord":"Core 0.3% vs 0.2%; PPI 6.5% y/y","src":"Bureau of Labor Statistics","trend":"down","trendWord":"Hot, no y/y","url":"https://www.bls.gov/news.release/cpi.nr0.htm"},{"change":"0 bp","latest":"3.75–4.00%","name":"Fed Funds Target","next":"9 Dec","period":"Held 28 Oct","prior":"3.50–3.75%","read":"Held at 3.75-4.00% on 28 Oct by 10-2 after the unanimous 25 bp hike of 16 Sept, with Cook and Hammack dissenting for a hike. The minutes released 18 Nov say almost all participants expect further increases and a couple would consider more than 25 bp in December if inflation disappoints; Williams said on 23 Nov he would not exclude consecutive meetings and Waller has called for 50 bp in December or a faster 2027 pace. The 25 Nov data gave the hawks what they needed: core PCE 3.3% against 3.2%, Q3 GDP 4.3%, claims 189K and a Beige Book with moderate wage growth and input costs outpacing prices. CME FedWatch per Investing.com priced 99.2% for a 25 bp hike on 9 Dec with the 50 bp option at zero, 60.1% for two hikes by 27 Jan, down from 67.4% on 24 Nov, and 35.2% for three by 17 Mar; Robinhood prediction markets priced 92% for December. The blackout began 28 Nov, so the 4 Dec jobs report is the last input. The decision comes 9 Dec with new projections.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Hold 10-2; Dec hike at 99.2% on FedWatch; two by January at 60.1%","src":"Federal Reserve","trend":"flat","trendWord":"Paused, 25 bp hike priced","url":"https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/pressreleases/monetary20261028a.htm"},{"change":"−$9.4B in a week","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":6.641},{"l":"Jan 26","v":6.588},{"l":"Feb 26","v":6.614},{"l":"Mar 26","v":6.657},{"l":"Apr 26","v":6.7},{"l":"May 26","v":6.704},{"l":"Jun 26","v":6.736},{"l":"Jul 26","v":6.738},{"l":"Aug 26","v":6.731},{"l":"Sep 26","v":6.743},{"l":"Oct 26","v":6.746},{"l":"25 Nov","v":6.732}],"latest":"$6.73T","name":"Fed Balance Sheet","next":"H.4.1 for 2 Dec, due 3 Dec","period":"25 Nov","prior":"$6.74T on 18 Nov","read":"Total assets were $6,732,044M on 25 Nov, down $9,408M on the week and up $179,625M on a year earlier, per the H.4.1 released 27 Nov after the holiday; the 18 Nov figure was $6,741,452M, down $17,415M. The balance sheet has moved within a $6.70T to $6.76T band since April, consistent with the end of runoff and reserve-management purchases offsetting the roll-off. The November point on the trend tabs is the 25 Nov reading. The sheet is not a policy signal now; the rate path is.","signal":"neutral","signalWord":"QT over","src":"Federal Reserve H.4.1; FRED WALCL","trend":"down","trendWord":"Flat near $6.74T","url":"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/WALCL"}],"sub":"Prices and the Federal Reserve's stance, read alongside the indicator set.","title":"Inflation and policy"},{"id":"markets","rows":[{"change":"+9 bp","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":4.18},{"l":"Jan 26","v":4.26},{"l":"Feb 26","v":3.97},{"l":"Mar 26","v":4.3},{"l":"Apr 26","v":4.4},{"l":"May 26","v":4.45},{"l":"Jun 26","v":4.44},{"l":"Jul 26","v":4.75},{"l":"Aug 26","v":4.75},{"l":"Sep 26","v":5.29},{"l":"Oct 26","v":4.94},{"l":"27 Nov","v":4.94}],"latest":"4.94%","name":"10-Year Treasury Yield","period":"27 Nov par close","prior":"4.85% par on 25 Nov","read":"The Treasury par 10-year rose 9 bp to 4.94% on 27 Nov, ending an eight-session fall from 5.07% on 18 Nov, as the haven bid faded with equities up 1.6%, Europe and Asia rallying on the tariff caps and China's market support, and December hike pricing firming to 99.2% on FedWatch. The 5-year rose 9 bp to 4.74%, the 7-year 8 bp to 4.82%, the 20-year 8 bp to 5.36% and the 30-year 9 bp to 5.33%. The yield is still 6 bp under the 5.00% Neutral condition and 35 bp under the 30 Sep close of 5.29%; the November point on the trend tabs is 4.94% until the 30 Nov close. FRED's DGS10 is posted to 25 Nov at 4.85%. The next supply is $177B of bills on 30 Nov; coupon auctions resume the week of 7 Dec with the FOMC in between.","signal":"neutral","signalWord":"Under the 5.00% Neutral condition; haven bid faded","src":"US Treasury par curve to 27 Nov; FRED DGS10 to 25 Nov","trend":"up","trendWord":"Eight-day fall ends","url":"https://home.treasury.gov/resource-center/data-chart-center/interest-rates/TextView?type=daily_treasury_yield_curve&field_tdr_date_value_month=202611"},{"change":"+8 bp","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":3.47},{"l":"Jan 26","v":3.52},{"l":"Feb 26","v":3.38},{"l":"Mar 26","v":3.79},{"l":"Apr 26","v":3.88},{"l":"May 26","v":3.98},{"l":"Jun 26","v":4.14},{"l":"Jul 26","v":4.28},{"l":"Aug 26","v":4.34},{"l":"Sep 26","v":4.88},{"l":"Oct 26","v":4.56},{"l":"27 Nov","v":4.69}],"latest":"4.69%","name":"2-Year Treasury Yield","period":"27 Nov par close","prior":"4.61% par on 25 Nov","read":"The par 2-year rose 8 bp to 4.69% on 27 Nov, retracing the 25 Nov fall and back above the 23 and 24 Nov closes of 4.63%, 69 bp above the 4.00% upper bound and so pricing a little under three 25 bp hikes. FedWatch per Investing.com has December at 99.2% with 50 bp at zero, two hikes by 27 Jan at 60.1%, down from 67.4% on 24 Nov, and three by 17 Mar at 35.2%, down from 51.7% on 16 Nov, so the front end has settled on December and is trading January on the 4 Dec jobs report, where a strong print would push January back toward 70-75% and a weak one makes it a coin flip per the same piece. The blackout started 28 Nov. Under 4.50% would say the market expects the correction to do the Fed's work; above 4.80% that the inflation arithmetic is unchanged.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Prices about three hikes; 99.2% for December","src":"US Treasury par curve to 27 Nov; CME FedWatch via Investing.com","trend":"up","trendWord":"Back to the 23 Nov level","url":"https://home.treasury.gov/resource-center/data-chart-center/interest-rates/TextView?type=daily_treasury_yield_curve&field_tdr_date_value_month=202611"},{"change":"+1 bp","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":71},{"l":"Jan 26","v":74},{"l":"Feb 26","v":59},{"l":"Mar 26","v":51},{"l":"Apr 26","v":52},{"l":"May 26","v":47},{"l":"Jun 26","v":30},{"l":"Jul 26","v":47},{"l":"Aug 26","v":41},{"l":"Sep 26","v":41},{"l":"Oct 26","v":38},{"l":"27 Nov","v":25}],"latest":"+25 bp","name":"2s10s Curve","period":"27 Nov par close","prior":"+24 bp par on 25 Nov","read":"The par 2s10s spread is 25 bp for 27 Nov, from 24 bp on 25 Nov, as the 10-year rose 9 bp and the 2-year 8 bp in a parallel sell-off; 5s30s is 59 bp. The curve has flattened from 41 bp at the end of September and 38 bp at the end of October as the front end priced the December and January hikes while the long end stopped rising, which is the market saying the hikes will slow growth rather than that inflation is unanchored. Under 20 bp would say the hikes are expected to bite; back above 35 bp with the long end leading would say the inflation premium is rebuilding. The 9 Dec projections give the committee's terminal rate against which the 2-year is priced.","ref":0,"refLabel":"0 = inversion line","signal":"warn","signalWord":"At the bottom of the 24-41 bp range","src":"FRED T10Y2Y; US Treasury par curve to 27 Nov","trend":"up","trendWord":"Flat near the June low","url":"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/T10Y2Y"},{"change":"+1.60%","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":6845.5},{"l":"Jan 26","v":6939.03},{"l":"Feb 26","v":6878.88},{"l":"Mar 26","v":6528.52},{"l":"Apr 26","v":7209.01},{"l":"May 26","v":7580.06},{"l":"Jun 26","v":7499.36},{"l":"Jul 26","v":7489.72},{"l":"Aug 26","v":7686.14},{"l":"Sep 26","v":7651.54},{"l":"Oct 26","v":7580.07},{"l":"27 Nov","v":7297.49}],"latest":"7,297.49","name":"S&P 500","period":"27 Nov close","prior":"7,182.78 on 25 Nov","read":"The S&P 500 rose 114.71 points or 1.60% to 7,297.49 in the 27 Nov half session, up 387.10 points or 5.6% on the week, the best week since April 2025, and up 451.99 points or 6.6% for the year per the AP. The Nasdaq rose 2.31% to 24,129.50, the Dow 1.18% to 50,774.94 and the Russell 2000 1.71% to 2,750.30; all 11 sector funds rose, more than four in five S&P 500 stocks were up, the median stock rose 0.86% and the equal-weight index about 1.3%, so breadth was broad rather than a megacap squeeze. The semiconductor index rose 4.69% with Sandisk up 15.96%, Micron 7.60%, Marvell 7.34% and Western Digital 7.22%, while Nvidia rose only 0.80% to $150.49, still 8.8% under Monday's $165.05; Anthropic rose 10.98% to $91.88, back above its $83 IPO price; Tesla rose 5.22%, AMD 4.56%, Amazon 3.96%, Broadcom 3.82%; Vertex fell 5.24% on an FDA review extension. The index is 6.7% under the 6 Oct record of 7,818.93 and 3.7% down for November with the 30 Nov session left, and 26 points under the 18 Nov close of 7,323.63 that would say 20 Nov was the low of this leg. A close back under 7,000 would say the rally was a squeeze; a VIX under 20 with the spread under 340 bp would move the posture.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"6.7% under the record; all 11 sectors up","src":"AP via myMotherLode; Milton Berg Edge; Stock Titan","trend":"up","trendWord":"Best week since April 2025","url":"https://mymotherlode.com/news/national/11344241/how-major-us-stock-indexes-fared-friday-11-27-2026.html"},{"change":"−4.48","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":14.95},{"l":"Jan 26","v":17.44},{"l":"Feb 26","v":19.86},{"l":"Mar 26","v":25.25},{"l":"Apr 26","v":16.89},{"l":"May 26","v":15.32},{"l":"Jun 26","v":16.45},{"l":"Jul 26","v":15.99},{"l":"Aug 26","v":14.92},{"l":"Sep 26","v":16.34},{"l":"Oct 26","v":19.42},{"l":"27 Nov","v":23.81}],"latest":"23.81","name":"VIX","period":"27 Nov close","prior":"28.29 on 25 Nov; FRED 26.80 for 26 Nov","read":"The VIX closed at 23.81 on 27 Nov per Milton Berg Edge and The Glass Desk, down 4.48 from the 25 Nov close of 28.29 and down 33.7% on the week from 35.89; The Company Chronicle quoted 24.01 at about 1:30 pm Eastern before the final print. FRED's VIXCLS now carries 26.80 for 26 Nov, which matches Saxo's 05:00 CET quote on 27 Nov from the global trading hours session, with the 27 Nov value due Monday. Saxo's early read had December VIX futures at 23.73 and January at 23.32 against spot 26.80, a backwardated curve that has now flattened with spot under 24, and VIX3M at 23.82 on Yahoo's figures sits level with spot. The index is still above the under-20 Neutral condition and above the 19.42 October close; it has closed under 20 on no day since 18 Nov. Under 20 with the S&P 500 above 7,323.63 would say the volatility regime has changed; a close back above 30 would say it has not.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Above the under-20 Neutral condition","src":"Cboe via Milton Berg Edge and The Glass Desk; FRED VIXCLS to 26 Nov","trend":"down","trendWord":"Lowest since 18 Nov","url":"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/VIXCLS"},{"change":"+5 bp","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":281},{"l":"Jan 26","v":288},{"l":"Feb 26","v":312},{"l":"Mar 26","v":328},{"l":"Apr 26","v":283},{"l":"May 26","v":274},{"l":"Jun 26","v":275},{"l":"Jul 26","v":285},{"l":"Aug 26","v":263},{"l":"Sep 26","v":312},{"l":"Oct 26","v":344},{"l":"26 Nov","v":377}],"latest":"377 bp","name":"High-Yield Credit Spread","next":"FRED posting for 27 Nov, due 30 Nov","period":"26 Nov","prior":"372 bp on 24 Nov","read":"FRED's ICE BofA US high-yield option-adjusted spread printed 3.77% for 25 Nov and again 3.77% for 26 Nov, 5 bp above the 24 Nov print of 3.72% and 1 bp above the 20 Nov peak of 3.76%, so credit made a new wide for this leg on the day the S&P 500 fell 0.25% and held it through the holiday. The 27 Nov print, due on FRED on 30 Nov, will show whether the 1.6% equity rally and the 4.69% semiconductor bounce pulled spreads in. The spread is 37 bp above the 340 bp Neutral condition and 27 bp above the 350 bp warning line, and 400 bp is the level that turns the AI equity correction into a credit story; CoreWeave five-year CDS was at a record 15.34% on 25 Nov with Nvidia, Oracle, Google and SpaceX CDS at records, and the 27 Nov digests had CoreWeave's 2030 notes near 76.5 cents for an 18.6% yield. The November point on the trend tabs is 377 bp.","signal":"bad","signalWord":"Above the 350 bp warning line; 377 on 25 and 26 Nov","src":"ICE BofA via FRED BAMLH0A0HYM2","trend":"up","trendWord":"New high for this leg","url":"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/BAMLH0A0HYM2"},{"change":"−$1.13","hist":[{"l":"12 Nov","v":95.82},{"l":"13 Nov","v":95.84},{"l":"16 Nov","v":92.44},{"l":"17 Nov","v":93.14},{"l":"18 Nov","v":91.95},{"l":"19 Nov","v":91.98},{"l":"20 Nov","v":92.57},{"l":"23 Nov","v":89.37},{"l":"24 Nov","v":91.4},{"l":"25 Nov","v":94.61},{"l":"26 Nov","v":96.57},{"l":"27 Nov","v":95.44}],"latest":"$95.44","name":"Brent Crude","period":"27 Nov settle","prior":"$96.57 on 26 Nov","read":"January Brent settled down $1.13 or 1.2% at $95.44 on 27 Nov after touching its highest since 9 Nov intraday, and WTI settled down $0.60 or 0.7% at $83.08 from Wednesday's $83.68 with no Thursday settlement; Brent rose about 3% on the week, its first weekly gain in November after three weekly falls, while WTI fell 0.9%. The bid came from a projectile that hit Aramco's 400,000 b/d Jazan refinery on 26 Nov, killing a Nepali worker and temporarily halting some operations per the Saudi Energy Ministry, with the coalition blaming a Houthi launch platform in Hodeidah that it then destroyed; the Houthi claim on Ras Tanura on 25 Nov, where Saudi forces intercepted four drones and no hit is confirmed; a drone attack in Kuwait that killed two Italian Rheinmetall technicians, which Iran denied; and UKMTO's report of a tanker struck 18 nautical miles east of Khasab. Against that the IEA projects a Q4 surplus, Gulf shipments are recovering, US crude stocks rose for a third week and three sources told CNBC that OPEC+ will hold Q1 2027 targets on 29 Nov while Iraq negotiates a higher ceiling; ANZ says Brent can clear $100 without another escalation only if deliverable supply proves constrained. FRED's Brent spot is posted to 24 Nov at $91.80, which the trend tabs carry. $100 is the relief line lost and $115 the trigger.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"$4.56 under the $100 relief line","src":"CNBC via The Circuitry and Political.org; FRED DCOILBRENTEU spot to 24 Nov","trend":"down","trendWord":"First weekly gain of November, +3%","url":"https://thecircuitry.to/article/brent-settles-friday-at-9544-and-wti-at-8308-mwzhz7en"},{"change":"+0.36%","latest":"104.04","name":"US Dollar Index","period":"27 Nov close","prior":"103.71 on 25 Nov per CappNotes","read":"The DXY closed at 104.04 on 27 Nov, up 0.37 points or 0.36% per Trading Economics, near its March 2025 highs and 27 bp under the 23 Nov close of 104.31, after a volatile week that ended little changed: the index slid for two sessions on falling Treasury yields and the haven bid into bonds, then recovered as yields rose 8 to 9 bp and December hike pricing reached 99.2%. The Glass Desk quotes 104.02. The drivers are the Fed against a BoJ hike priced at 79% for 18 Dec and an ECB hike at 92% for 17 Dec, so the dollar is strong on rates rather than on growth. Brazil's real jumped from 5.88 to 5.53 per dollar after Lula named Bradesco chairman Trabuco finance minister. FRED's broad dollar index is posted to 20 Nov at 123.60, which the trend tabs carry; the next FRED update is 30 Nov. A rising dollar with rising yields tightens conditions for dollar borrowers in the GCC, Asia and emerging markets; the 20-month high of 104.31 on 23 Nov is the level to watch.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Near the March 2025 high","src":"Trading Economics; The Glass Desk; FRED DTWEXBGS to 20 Nov","trend":"up","trendWord":"Back toward the 23 Nov high","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/united-states/currency"},{"change":"−0.3 pt","latest":"2.9%","name":"Atlanta Fed GDPNow, Q4","next":"1 Dec","period":"25 Nov estimate","prior":"3.2% on 18 Nov","read":"GDPNow stands at 2.9% for Q4 after the 25 Nov cut from 3.2% when real October spending printed flat; the Atlanta Fed made no update on 27 Nov and the next is 1 Dec after ISM manufacturing and construction spending. The 27 Nov advance indicators pull in opposite directions: the goods deficit widened $14.4B to $149.5B as imports rose $21.1B, which subtracts from net exports, while wholesale inventories rose 0.9% and retail inventories 0.8%, which adds to inventory investment. Q3 came in at 4.3% on the second estimate with real final sales to private domestic purchasers at 5.8%, so 2.9% for Q4 is a slowdown to trend, not a stall; under 2.5% after the ISM round would say Q4 is losing the Q3 momentum.","signal":"good","signalWord":"Near-trend growth","src":"Atlanta Fed","trend":"down","trendWord":"Cut after flat real spending","url":"https://www.atlantafed.org/cqer/research/gdpnow"}],"sub":"Closing levels for Wednesday 25 Nov unless a date is shown. Yields are the Treasury par curve posted for 25 Nov; the VIX close is CappNotes' and Yahoo Finance's, with FRED posted to 24 Nov at 26.36. The high-yield spread is FRED's 24 Nov print of 372 bp; the 25 Nov figure is due 27 Nov.","title":"Market readings"}],"takeaways":["The S&P 500 closed at 7,297.49 on 27 Nov, up 114.71 points or 1.60% in the 1 pm half session, up 387.10 points or 5.6% on the week, the best week since April 2025, and up 6.6% for the year; the Nasdaq rose 2.31% to 24,129.50, the Dow 1.18% to 50,774.94 and the Russell 2000 1.71% to 2,750.30, with all 11 sectors up, more than four in five S&P 500 stocks higher and the semiconductor index up 4.69% on the day and 9.25% on the week per the AP and Milton Berg Edge. The index is still 6.7% under the 6 Oct record of 7,818.93 and 3.7% down for November with one session left; the VIX closed at 23.81, down 33.7% on the week.","The bond market gave back the 25 Nov rally: the Treasury par curve posted the 2-year at 4.69%, the 5-year at 4.74%, the 10-year at 4.94% and the 30-year at 5.33% for 27 Nov, up 8 to 9 bp across the curve, with 2s10s at 25 bp. The 10-year stays under the 5.00% Neutral condition for a fifth session. FedWatch per Investing.com priced 99.2% for a 25 bp hike on 9 Dec, 60.1% for a second by 27 Jan, down from 67.4% on 24 Nov, and 35.2% for a third by March; the Fed blackout began on 28 Nov.","Credit did not confirm the equity rally. FRED's ICE BofA high-yield spread printed 377 bp for 25 Nov and again for 26 Nov, 5 bp above the 24 Nov print and 1 bp above the 20 Nov peak of 376 bp, so the spread made a new high for this leg on the days the index was rallying; the 27 Nov print is due Monday. CoreWeave, Nvidia and Oracle CDS stayed at or near records per the 27 Nov digests. 400 bp is the line that turns the equity correction into a credit story.","Oil eased into the OPEC+ meeting but kept a weekly gain: Brent settled down $1.13 or 1.2% at $95.44 and WTI down $0.60 at $83.08 on 27 Nov, Brent up about 3% on the week, its first weekly gain in November, after a projectile hit Aramco's 400,000 b/d Jazan refinery on 26 Nov, killing a Nepali worker and halting some operations per the Saudi Energy Ministry, the coalition intercepted four drones aimed at Ras Tanura on 25 Nov, a drone strike in Kuwait killed two Italian Rheinmetall technicians, and UKMTO logged a tanker hit 18 nautical miles east of Khasab; three sources told CNBC that OPEC+ will keep Q1 2027 targets unchanged on 29 Nov while Iraq negotiates a higher ceiling.","Data on 27 Nov were second-tier: the advance October goods deficit widened $14.4B to $149.5B as imports rose $21.1B to $365.3B and exports $6.7B to $215.8B, which subtracts from Q4 net exports, while wholesale inventories rose 0.9% and retail inventories 0.8%, which adds to Q4 investment; GDPNow stays at 2.9% until 1 Dec. The Fed's H.4.1 for 25 Nov showed total assets down $9.4B to $6.732T. FRED's ADXTNO level for October is 226,726, 11.4% above October 2025, so the durables year-on-year point on the trend tabs is filled. Next week: ISM manufacturing and JOLTS on 1 Dec with a 55 consensus, ISM services and claims on 3 Dec, and payrolls on 4 Dec with a 90K LSEG consensus and 4.1% unemployment."],"translation":[{"body":"The S&P 500 closed at 7,297.49 on 27 Nov, up 1.60% in the half session and 5.6% on the week, the best week since April 2025, with all 11 sectors up, more than four in five stocks higher and the equal-weight index up about 1.3%, so this leg of the rebound was broad; the semiconductor index rose 4.69% on memory names with Sandisk up 15.96% and Micron 7.60% while Nvidia rose only 0.80% to $150.49, still 8.8% under Monday, so the market is buying AI hardware demand and not the anchor company's governance. Under the framework this is a correction in an Expansion: the index is 6.7% under the 6 Oct record, 3.7% down for November and 26 points under the 7,323.63 line that would say 20 Nov was the low, with the VIX at 23.81 above 20 and the high-yield spread at 377 bp making a new high for this leg on 25 and 26 Nov. Posture stays Risk-off on two of three conditions. The next test is 1 to 4 Dec: ISM manufacturing with a 55 consensus, Huang's House interviews and Senate testimony, and payrolls with a 90K consensus; a clean ISM and a payroll print near 100K with the VIX under 20 would move the posture to Neutral if credit follows, while a close back under 7,000 would say the rally was a squeeze.","title":"Equities"},{"body":"The Treasury par curve sold off 8 to 9 bp on 27 Nov: the 2-year at 4.69%, the 5-year at 4.74%, the 10-year at 4.94% and the 30-year at 5.33%, with 2s10s at 25 bp. The 25 Nov rally was a haven bid and it reversed when equities bounced, Europe and Asia rallied on the tariff caps and China's market-support statement, and FedWatch moved to 99.2% for December; the 10-year is still under the 5.00% Neutral condition for a fifth session but the eight-session fall is over. The front end has settled December and is trading January on the 4 Dec jobs report: two hikes by 27 Jan at 60.1%, down from 67.4% on 24 Nov, and three by March at 35.2%, so a 90K payroll print leaves January near a coin flip and a 150K print pushes it back toward 70%. Credit is the lead risk and it diverged from equities: FRED's high-yield spread printed 377 bp for both 25 and 26 Nov, above the 376 bp peak of 20 Nov, with CoreWeave's 2030 notes near an 18.6% yield and Nvidia and Oracle CDS at records; the 27 Nov print on 30 Nov is the tell, under 350 bp the first relief and above 400 bp the credit story. Supply is $177B of bills on 30 Nov, then coupons after the FOMC; the Treasury had $511.1B of debt-limit room on 24 Nov per Saxo.","title":"Fixed income"},{"body":"The committee goes into the blackout with October core PCE at 3.3%, headline 3.8%, Q3 GDP at 4.3%, claims at 189K and a Beige Book of moderate wage and price gains, so 25 bp on 9 Dec is priced at 99.2% with the 50 bp option at zero after Waller's call for it; the argument is January, where the futures price 60.1% for a second hike and the 4 Dec payrolls decide the lean. Bessent's 22 Nov warning that the Fed risks over-tightening is the political counterweight, and the Cook hearing on 14 Dec follows the meeting. The balance sheet fell $9.4B to $6.732T in the week to 25 Nov with runoff over, so liquidity is a rates story, not a reserves story. Trade policy is a 2027 story: USTR's Section 301 proposals are capped at the 15% trade-deal rates for the EU, Japan, Korea, Taiwan and Switzerland, 20% on China with a 40% alternative, with comments due 5 Jan, a hearing on 25 Jan and the US-China truce expiring 10 Jan. Oil at $95.44 after a 3% weekly gain on the Jazan strike and Kuwait drone deaths is the input that can move the inflation call before CPI on 10 Dec; OPEC+ holding Q1 2027 targets on 29 Nov keeps supply unchanged. The DXY at 104.04 and gold futures at $3,668.50, up about 2% on the day, say the market is paying for both tighter dollar policy and a hedge against the war.","title":"Liquidity and policy"}]};
